(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-easy-2026-005"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-easy-2026-005","title":"Basic Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","sourceTitle":"Basic Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering","seriesId":"cs-easy-2026","num":5,"category":"basic","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Computer_Science_Engineering/009_Basic Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>n</em>! = 1 × 2 × 3 × _ _ _ × <em>n</em> for integer <em>n</em> ≥ 1. If <em>P</em> = (1 × 1!) + (2 × 2!) + (3 × 3!) + ______ (10 × 10!),<br />\nthen <em>P</em> + 2 when divided by 11! leaves a remainder of</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"10"},{"key":"B","html":"0"},{"key":"C","html":"7"},{"key":"D","html":"1"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6b10b4e74da14e267b1d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Δ<em>ABC</em> and Δ<em>DBC</em> have a common base and drawn towards the same side, ∠<em>BAC</em> = ∠<em>BDC</em> = 90°. If <em>AC</em> and <em>DB</em> intersect at <em>P</em>, then</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23fbbd45d36d44282961.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>AP</em> × <em>PC</em> = <em>BP</em> × <em>PD</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>AP</em> × <em>BP</em> = <em>PC</em> × <em>PD</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>AP</em> × <em>PD</em> = <em>PC</em> × <em>BP</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea8923f9ebbc34b13419.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/6476af87eaa76cb40b73.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>the possible value(s) of <em>x</em> is/are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/def93987ae5c3340d6f4.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa950fe5f9a688a263c5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"3"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/3382afef9792c32123a4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f3664e511f282d80f70.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"0"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>abc</em>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fcd72e8c92adccd0568e.png\" /></div>\n</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In the adjoining diagram, <em>ABCD</em> is a square with side ‘<em>a</em>’ cm. The area of the larger circle with centre ‘<em>O</em>’ is equal to the sum of the areas of the remaining four circles with equal radii, whose centres are <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em> and <em>S</em>. What is the ratio between the side of square and radius of a smaller circle?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c732cfa2820c208d4ef2.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7a29ce11e1655e673857.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/92/92bbdc422eeec47d1571.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c6e8f87f4bd66e10dbde.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"Can’t be determined"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e1c820ea2314a4f9fd8e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8fcf9552703912e92c8a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The question below consists of a question and two statements numbered I and II given below it.<br />\nYou have to decide whether the data provided in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Read both the statements and give answer<br />\n<em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em>, <em>S</em> and <em>T</em> are sitting around a circular table facing towards the centre. Who among them is sitting to the immediate right of <em>R</em>?<br />\nI. <em>Q</em> is sitting between <em>P</em> and <em>T</em>, and <em>T</em> is sitting to the immediate right of <em>S</em>.<br />\nII. <em>Q</em> is second to the left of <em>R</em> and is second to the right of <em>S</em>.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The data in statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, while the data in statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question;"},{"key":"B","html":"The data in statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, while the data in statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question;"},{"key":"C","html":"The data either in statement I alone or in statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question;"},{"key":"D","html":"The data given in both statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/871e8683fe34a8f03981.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If the <em>p</em><sup>th</sup> term of an <em>AP</em> is <em>q</em> and its <em>q</em><sup>th</sup> term is p, then its mth term is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91ee822ad75a6176e0da.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/beb3b38c63bfc9d31a15.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d8bb279f95e8283ac807.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/1502feb64c379d5c853c.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f3fe35f73ef7acb2212.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In the following question, a part of the sentence is given in bold. Choose the correct alternative that best improves the bold part. If no improvement is needed, select “No improvement.”<br />\nThe Vice Chancellor congratulated the blind student <strong>for his</strong> success in the examination.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"in his"},{"key":"B","html":"on his"},{"key":"C","html":"over his"},{"key":"D","html":"No improvement"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe verb &ldquo;congratulate&rdquo; is typically followed by the preposition &ldquo;on&rdquo; when referring to the reason for the congratulation.<br />\nCorrect sentence: &ldquo;The Vice Chancellor congratulated the blind student on his success in the examination.&rdquo;</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Read the passage carefully and choose the best answer to the following question from the four alternatives given below.<br />\nIn the past 50 years, doctors across the world have accepted the practice to prescribe antibiotics at the first sign of a trivial infection or treat patients with a handful of antibiotics. These days it is not uncommon to see practitioners prescribing multiple antibiotics without any real indication or relevance for such a combination of drugs. Antibiotics have traditionally been known as miracle drugs, but there is growing evidence that they are overworked miracles, especially in countries like ours where there is easy access to drugs across the counter, including antibiotics. We cannot think of a return to pre-antibiotic days. Yet the unbridled use of these agents is inexorably propelling us in that direction.<br />\nAntibiotics are called overworked miracles because:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"they performed miracles."},{"key":"B","html":"they are hardly used."},{"key":"C","html":"they are over-used."},{"key":"D","html":"they exhausted their miracles."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe passage clearly points out that antibiotics are being excessively used, so they are called overworked miracles.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>For the mirror represented by a line MN, choose the correct mirror-image of the figure, from amongst the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given along with it.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c27179bb94658d842a48.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/9691568d1f87f20e414e.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1e/1e507a01ad33df7415a7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/aba2e751240b17b5a74e.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9a64d7a92572c7cf729b.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A digital circuit is having the output <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a7b756a17c6fed7d010f.png\" /> If produces the output as 1, if</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>A</em> ≠ <em>B</em> and <em>C</em> may be any value"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>A</em> ≠ <em>C</em> and <em>B</em> may be any value"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>A</em> = <em>B</em> and <em>C</em> may be any value"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>A</em> = <em>C</em> and <em>B</em> may be any value"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIf <em>A</em> = <em>B</em>, then <em>f</em> = 1.<br />\nSo <em>C</em> may be any value.</p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following automata:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1adc337327d963f934bf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following regular expression is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0* (110*) + 11"},{"key":"B","html":"(1* (101*)* + 11"},{"key":"C","html":"1(110*)*"},{"key":"D","html":"(1011*)*"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the undirected graph G defined as follows, the vertices are bit string of length 5. We have an edge between vertex “S” and vertex “T” iff “S” and “T” differ only in one bit (i.e. hamming distance) what is the ratio of chromatic number of graph G to the diameter of graph G?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/71add24ea2162b670783.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d4e8caec18524949307.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c100f7ccdb94f0b91018.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6ad2f01106351ed092d4.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa0df84a7220e8fc51ad.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85e32cfdbd6666cd8d86.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"4"},{"key":"C","html":"8"},{"key":"D","html":"Limit does not exists"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7de3047358c95d052ca7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>What type of socket is used by Socket API if UDP Protocol has to be used in transport Layer?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"STREAM-SOCK"},{"key":"B","html":"DGRAM-SOCK"},{"key":"C","html":"RAW-Sock"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A process in the suspended blocked state moves to the suspended ready state if the event it was waiting for completes but it is still on secondary storage."},{"key":"B","html":"A ready process may be preempted and moved back to the waiting state without executing."},{"key":"C","html":"SRTF always gives less Number of context switches compare to Round Robin."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A certain microprocessor required 5.7 microseconds to respond to an interrupt. Assuming that the three interrupts <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/58/58e24b1a850185c5c3e4.png\" /> require the following execution time after the interrupt is recognized.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f7ae19641867bf25853.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/570cb8d6f459af0c8eb0.png\" /> has the highest priority and <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a9a20abc96cb0c9aae3a.png\" /> has the lowest. What is the possible range of time for <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/86/8632e5bf618ca2be1b1e.png\" /> to be executed assuming that it may or may not occur simultaneously with other interrupts?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"20.7 μsec and 35.7 μsec"},{"key":"B","html":"35.7 μsec and 82.1 μsec"},{"key":"C","html":"20.7 μsec and 82.1 μsec"},{"key":"D","html":"20.7 μsec and 80.7 μsec"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c3/c3e143de0c2df4119df7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"In abstract syntax tree all operators appears at internal nodes instead of leaves nodes."},{"key":"B","html":"For any input program, abstract syntax tree can contain cycle."},{"key":"C","html":"Abstract syntax tree omitted the semantic details of parse tree."},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; In abstract syntax tree all operators appears at internal nodes instead of leaves nodes.<br />\n&bull; AST is tree and tree can never contain a cycle.<br />\n&bull; AST omitted the syntactic details of parse tree but not semantic.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following recurrence relation</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/3091ea1a6bd20a51db09.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/34fb99a88bc14dc42de3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59e4458b420002df5209.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/5720e3ba64286073b11e.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7c/7cb93adcaa51d6d4d507.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/38/38221cf4d7195334a8fd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/05/05e45099b80077f47b76.png\" /> be two relations in which a is the foreign key of <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> that refers to the primary key of <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> . Consider following four options.<br />\nI. Insert into <em>R</em><sub>1</sub><br />\nII. Insert into <em>R<sub>2</sub></em><br />\nIII. Delete from <em>R</em><sub>1</sub><br />\nIV. Delete from <em>R</em><sub>2</sub><br />\nWhich of the following is correct about the referential integrity constraint with respect to above?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Operations (I) and (II) will cause violation."},{"key":"B","html":"Operations (II) and (III) will cause violation."},{"key":"C","html":"Operations (III) and (IV) will cause violation."},{"key":"D","html":"Operations (IV) and (I) will cause violation."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/4382773c002479fbd575.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is NOT supported by TCP sockets?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Unicasting"},{"key":"B","html":"Broadcasting"},{"key":"C","html":"Both (a) and (b)"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is false?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The Halting problem of Turing Machines is decidable."},{"key":"B","html":"Determining whether a context-free grammar is ambiguous is undecidable."},{"key":"C","html":"Given two arbitrary context-free grammars <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub> it is undecidable whether <em>L</em>(<em>G</em><sub>1</sub>) = <em>L</em>(<em>G</em><sub>2</sub>)."},{"key":"D","html":"Given two regular grammar <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub> it is undecidable whether <em>L</em>(<em>G</em><sub>1</sub>) = <em>L</em>(<em>G</em><sub>2</sub>)."}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)<br />\nThe Halting problem of TM is undecidable. Equivalence of regular languages is decidable.</p>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The result of given arithmetic expression is <em>X</em>, it is in 8 bit 2’s complement form, a where <em>X</em> is (104)<sub>6</sub> – (101)<sub>5</sub></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11110010"},{"key":"B","html":"10001110"},{"key":"C","html":"00001010"},{"key":"D","html":"11111110"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/997ae0eee5ec312d7ce0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Socket( ) system call is created at client side</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"then client is in connected state"},{"key":"B","html":"client is in connectionless state"},{"key":"C","html":"Endpoint address is created"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following set of processes that need to be scheduled on a single CPU.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d3422e375f2574299988.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What is the throughput of the system if the SRTF CPU scheduling algorithm used?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.2,0.2],"answerText":"0.2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d986ca46baf98338976d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Assume that ADD <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/19ff286fbbb9f3e650f2.png\" /> denotes x ← y ∗ z. Consider the following assembly code.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d002456c9aa6d9cb1ba.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Find the number of RAW dependencies in the above assembly code.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"5"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"6"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/48/48dbdaac9f1c5356bcab.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Identify the correct statement when LR parsers compared using any particular grammar.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If LR (0) has no conflict then SLR (1) may have the conflicts"},{"key":"B","html":"If SLR (1) has conflict then LR (0) never contain any conflict"},{"key":"C","html":"If LALR (1) has conflict then CLR (1) never contain any conflict"},{"key":"D","html":"If CLR (1) has no conflict then LALR (1) may have the conflicts"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nIf grammar is LR (0) then it is also SLR (1). It implies if no conflict present in LR (0) then SLR (1)<br />\nalso don&rsquo;t have any conflict.<br />\nIf CLR (1) has no conflict then LALR (1) may have SR conflict.<br />\nHence option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Given a weighted directed graph with n vertices where edge weights are integers (positive, zero or negative) determining whether there are paths of arbitrarily large weight can be performed in time.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5b1717b91ab0c20334d3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/ba2c37e21726e9ae32b5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/549e2d9bf009abdffdbc.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4f/4f87dba75ff2ecbaf939.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea92a3cb9894cb9facd3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Assume the relations <em>X</em>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) and <em>Y</em>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) and consider the following query.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/72/72a2987f73b2e3766828.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the above query results in <em>X</em> ∩ <em>Y</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"I only"},{"key":"B","html":"II only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both I and II"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c462b8319a65e29d625.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Shriti and Saloni are throwing an unbiased six-faced die alternately. Whoever throws a number ≥ 5 first wins the game. If Shriti throws the die first, what is the probability that Saloni wins the game?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.32,0.45],"answerText":"0.4","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.4 [0.32 - 0.45]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/417cfa460b6cc25d1d4a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The number of states which are not reachable states in the given counter circuit, ________.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/01465dd7be4706d52c44.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f6/f62564bdf8a6fc1849de.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is true regarding traffic shaping?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It is achieved when the input traffic is always bursty."},{"key":"B","html":"It ensures a constant output rate from the router, regardless of the input traffic."},{"key":"C","html":"It is a method of losing packets when the router cannot handle them."},{"key":"D","html":"It prioritizes new packets over old packets for applications like FTP."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two processes <em>P<sub>i</sub></em> and <em>P<sub>j</sub></em>, need to access a critical section of code. Consider the following synchronization construct used by the two processes:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bc1ec307bc7b6f8ed6ae.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>\" Turn is a shared variable between both the processes \"</div>\n\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5f/5f3da3a49f42dc7e489e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which one of the following statements is true about the construct?</div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It does not ensure mutual exclusion."},{"key":"B","html":"The process that executes statement-1 will always be the first one to enter the critical section."},{"key":"C","html":"It doesn’t prevent deadlocks, but ensure mutual exclusion."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/aca8bafd47d603bc52b8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Two pipeline <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>2</sub> are implemented by a system such that <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> has 5 phases with uniform delay of 3 ns and buffer delay of 2 ns. While <em>P</em><sub>2</sub> has 6 phases with stage delay as 3 ns, 1 ns, 4 ns, 2 ns, 1 ns, and 3 ns with buffer delay of 2 ns .If the buffer delay in <em>P</em><sub>2</sub> can be reduced by half in the enhancement of <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>. How much additional time is saved for executing 150 instruction in enhanced pipe <em>P</em><sub>2</sub> over old pipe <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[155,155],"answerText":"155","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>155</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e9db8eaee504463d0b7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a directed graph with vertex set {0, 1, 2, 3}. The weight matrix <em>W</em> is calculated such that, entry <em>W<sub>ij</sub></em> depicts the weight of the edge {i, j} Matrix <em>W<sub>ij</sub></em> is computed as,</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/43cf1299a769573230da.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The minimum possible weight of a spanning tree <em>T</em> of the graph such that vertex 1 is a leaf node in the tree T is ________.</div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-24,-24],"answerText":"-24","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>-24</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/108ffe0511742c505e3b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the below statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Life time of dynamically allocated memory in heap segment is entire program.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : NULL pointer dereferencing error and dangling pointer both are same.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Register variables (storage class) scope is entire program.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>4</sub> : In void pointer’s type casting mandatory while dereferencing the data.<br />\nHow many statements are correct _________?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2</p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : A relation R in 1NF can have multiple foreign keys.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : A relation R in 1NF can have multivalued attributes.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Dependency-preserving BCNF decomposition may not be possible for all relations.<br />\nWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are correct"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> are correct"},{"key":"C","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> are correct"},{"key":"D","html":"All <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> are correct"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is true about stack ADT implemented using linked list?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"For push ( ) operation new nodes are inserted at the head of linked list. For pop ( ) operation node are removed from head only."},{"key":"B","html":"For push ( ) operation new nodes are inserted at the head of linked list. For pop ( ) operation node are removed from rear end of the linked list."},{"key":"C","html":"For push ( ) operation new nodes are inserted at the rear end of the linked list. For pop ( ) operation node are removed from head of linked list."},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nFor push ( ) operation new nodes are inserted at the head of linked list. For pop ( ) operation<br />\nnode are removed from head only.</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following floating point format:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64b825a98a9c9a87f9ab.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The decimal number (–42.375) has following hexadecimal representation with normalization and rounding off</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"C4229800"},{"key":"B","html":"C2298000"},{"key":"C","html":"43298000"},{"key":"D","html":"CB229800"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4ebc0de6d901dab9c077.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the graph given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/81136e44895a506e152b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Suppose you are running Dijkstra’s algorithm starting from some source vertex <em>D</em>. The table given below shows the shortest path tree immediately after vertex <em>E</em> has been relaxed.</div>\n\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/78/78ddd7fc026f65a724dd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following vertex is NOT relaxed before <em>E</em>?</div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>F</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>B</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>H</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b2/b2e5c3273995e964428f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following represents regular language?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e862c5d701c9d27f7f2f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/45aaebd7ace85ade10a9.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"Both (a) and (b)"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e5/e54d13565cae49abd7ab.png\" /></div>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following code:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/be8304136efd92592598.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The value returned by the function “find” when a pointer to the root of a non-empty tree is passed as argument is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The number of levels in the tree"},{"key":"B","html":"The number of nodes in the tree"},{"key":"C","html":"The number of internal nodes in the tree"},{"key":"D","html":"The height of the tree"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nThe given program recursively find the number of levels in left sub tree and number of levels in<br />\nright sub tree of root node, then at last it will add 1 + max (levels in left sub tree, levels in right sub tree).<br />\nWhich will result number of levels in given tree.</p>"},{"n":43,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Given a partially ordered set <em>X</em> represented by Hasse diagram below, let <em>X<sup>d</sup></em> be the dual poset formed by reversing all the order relations of <em>X</em>. Determine the number of topological sort of the dual poset <em>X<sup>d</sup></em>.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ee/ee06ddb524456521f8a7.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>12</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d7e0baa0ece40cc99a38.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a63dc54b4699c7e99dcd.png\" /> then which of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bfa71bfd36926359681c.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/0374f00cb8df950d5de9.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/6017d016289ade05ccae.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/84/846c061ec4943f09637f.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/11/1101988affc3ad802782.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which system call will be helpful to know local Endpoint address of bounded Socket at server side?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Socket( )"},{"key":"B","html":"Bind( )"},{"key":"C","html":"connect( )"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A multi-threaded application contains three threads <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>T</em><sub>3</sub> within a single process<br />\nConsider the following events:<br />\nI. <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> performs a blocking I/O operation.<br />\nII. The operating system provides no kernel-level thread support (threads are entirely at user level).<br />\nIII. A thread scheduler is implemented within the process.<br />\nWhich of the following statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"T2 and T3 will continue executing while <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> waits for I/O completion."},{"key":"B","html":"All threads in the process will be blocked until <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>’s I/O completes."},{"key":"C","html":"The OS can directly switch from <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> to <em>T</em><sub>2</sub>."},{"key":"D","html":"The OS will preemptively schedule <em>T</em><sub>3</sub> when <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> is blocked."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; With user-level threads, the OS is unaware of the existence of threads &mdash; it only sees the process.<br />\n&bull; If one thread makes a blocking system call, the entire process blocks, causing all threads in<br />\nthat process to stop until the blocking operation finishes.</p>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider two different implementations of the same instruction set architecture, <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>.<br />\nProcessor <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> runs on a clock rate of 1.5 GHz and <em>P</em><sub>2</sub> runs on 2.5 GHz. There are four classes of instructions A, B, C and D. The CPI’s of each implementation are given in the following table.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d2/d2e414c7a5d696a1bd7f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Given a program with 106 instructions divided into 4 classes according to the frequencies in the above table. Choose the correct statement from the following.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>P</em><sub>1</sub> is faster than <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>P</em><sub>2</sub> is faster than <em>P</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>P</em><sub>1</sub> is same as <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/1847c70604ef536618e6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the insertion sort procedure given below, which sorts an array <em>L</em> of size <em>n</em> (≥ 2) in ascending order:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fb9d72611415177b4fdb.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>It is known that insertion sort makes atmost <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d59677293501b773fe78.png\" /> comparisons. Which of the following is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"There is no input on which insertion sort makes <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4561f01cba2e395bc1b.png\" /> comparisons."},{"key":"B","html":"Insertion sort makes <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/49/49080de17316a7a3fe94.png\" /> comparisons when the input is already sorted in ascending order."},{"key":"C","html":"Insertion sort makes <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7ec2910c3c990878565d.png\" /> comparisons when the input is sorted in descending order."},{"key":"D","html":"Insertion sort makes <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5a88d19dfa82b9c665a8.png\" /> comparisons whenever all of the elements of <em>L</em> are not distinct."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/94982bf67e65a05f5f7e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the regular expression <em>R</em> = (<em>a</em> + <em>b</em>)*<em>a</em>(<em>ab</em>)*+ ε. Which of the following are possible as subset of <em>R</em>?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/57d2c05935d901e28eaa.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(i) and (ii) only"},{"key":"B","html":"(ii) and (iii) only"},{"key":"C","html":"(i), (ii) and (iii)"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/69f0bf81292ffa263cdb.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the converse of the following assertion?<br />\n“I can win the game only if I practice”</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"I can win the game if I practice."},{"key":"B","html":"If I can win the game then I practice."},{"key":"C","html":"If I do not practice then I can not win the game."},{"key":"D","html":"If I can not win the game then I practice."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/61/61854fdbd6de25cb4020.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>k</em> be a positive real number and let</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/016edd87c2b3e30f4e3b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If det (Adj <em>A</em>) + det (adj <em>B</em>) = 10<sup>6</sup>, then <em>k</em> is equal to __________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4.5,4.5],"answerText":"4.5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4.5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/693f1eacbf734dfd600a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A computer system uses a 32 bit physical address and a 35 bit virtual address. It implements twolevel paging for memory management. Each page table entry occupies 4 bytes, and every page table fits exactly into one page. The first-level page table holds the base addresses of second-level page tables, and each second-level page table holds entries that map virtual pages to physical frames. What is the page size in KB for this system?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/fa80c321ba031f10d6e3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following code for single linked list:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a2594c47ec26999cd25.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If head is a pointer to a pointer to the first node of the list and it is passed to the ‘Modified’ function then find the list after executing the function.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It adds a new node at the first"},{"key":"B","html":"It adds a new node at the last"},{"key":"C","html":"It keeps the list as same"},{"key":"D","html":"It reverses the list"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/990b67d6ddfddfb88582.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider an ordered file with 112000 records stored on a disk using un-spanned file organization block size is 4096 bytes and record length 512 bytes. If primary indexing is used over a field of size 9 bytes and block pointer size is 7 bytes. Then number of block access is required to search<br />\nfor a record is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[7,7],"answerText":"7","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>7</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/4351832d32da8a66bee1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The function <em>f</em>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) realised by the given multiplexer is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d24145464fd8acdee19.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"having 5 maxterms"},{"key":"B","html":"having 4 minterms"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em> minimal expression is <em>AB</em> + <em>BC</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>f </em>= 0"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is false?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A language recognized by a non-deterministic Turing Machine can also be recognized by a deterministic Turing Machine."},{"key":"B","html":"Complement of a context free language can be recognized by a Turing Machine."},{"key":"C","html":"If a language and its complement are both recursively enumerable then it is recursive."},{"key":"D","html":"Complement of a non recursive language can never be recognized by any Turing Machine."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n(a) Non-deterministic TM and deterministic TM are equivalent.<br />\n(b) Complement of a CFL is recursive and recursive language is recognized by Turing Machine.<br />\nSo, complement of a context free language can be recognized by a TM.<br />\n(c) If a language is recursively enumerable, there exists a TM which outputs &ldquo;yes&rdquo; whenever a<br />\nstring is in the language. If its complement is recursively enumerable, there exists a TM which<br />\noutputs &ldquo;yes&rdquo; whenever the string is not in the language. So, we can have a &ldquo;yes&rdquo; if a string<br />\nbelongs to the language and &ldquo;no&rdquo; if a string does not belong to the language &rArr; language is recursive.<br />\n(d) If a language is non-recursively enumerable, its complement may be recursively enumerable<br />\n(can be recognized by a Turing Machine).</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Any protocol is applied between as per TCP/IP model</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Sender’s Network layer and receiver’s data link Layer"},{"key":"B","html":"Sender’s Network layer and receiver’s Transport Layer"},{"key":"C","html":"Sender’s Network layer and receiver’s Network layer"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1ac6748b1433324f46c6.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[25,25],"answerText":"25","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>25</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d88fef62fa7780c6e257.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"In write-through with no-write-allocate, a cache write miss results in data being written directly to main memory without loading the block into the cache."},{"key":"B","html":"In write-back, modified cache blocks are written to secondary memory only when they are replaced."},{"key":"C","html":"Write-allocate loads the block into cache on a write miss before performing the write."},{"key":"D","html":"A cache that uses a write-back policy with write-allocate will load the block into cache on a write miss and defer writing to main memory until the block is replaced."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\n&bull; In write-through + no-write-allocate, a write miss writes directly to main memory without<br />\ncaching the block. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; In write-back, dirty blocks are written to main memory, not to secondary storage, when<br />\nreplaced. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Write-allocate fetches the block into the cache on a write miss before updating it. So, option<br />\n(c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Write-back + write-allocate loads the block on a write miss and delays main memory update<br />\nuntil block replacement. So, option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements with respect to ACID Atomicity consistency isolation and durability) properties which one is not true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Atomicity and durability is maintained by recovery management system of DBMS software."},{"key":"B","html":"Isolation is maintained by concurrency control component of DBMS software."},{"key":"C","html":"Atomicity means execute all operation of transaction (or) execute none of operation, but execution of commit is not required while executing all operation."},{"key":"D","html":"Consistency in usually maintained by the user itself."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/19357f144590910f111f.png\" /></div>\n</div>"},{"n":61,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In particular unix OS each data block is of size 1024 bytes each node has 10 direct data blocks and 2-additional addresses one for single indirect block and one for double indirect block. Each block is addressed with 64 byte. The maximum possible size of file in the file system<br />\n(in KB) is _________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[282,282],"answerText":"282","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>282</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f89d3978c5c9a153c150.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following augmented grammar with labels a and b:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33c01892673511673d02.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If the following SLR (1) table is constructed as below then find the missing entries at <em>E</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>E</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>E</em><sub>3 </sub>respectively.\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e94a1a5ab75564354922.png\" /></div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/17/173f8e7bc5dc2da880a2.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5dde2bc3eb7b98bec96b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0dafabec3f0b7824005a.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da640724aac3ab1c3ba9.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/697f90b840e1cef73c33.png\" /></div>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following implementation of SELECTION SORT to sort the array A in a nondecreasing<br />\norder. The algorithm first finds the smallest element and places in the first location.<br />\nThen repeatedly selects the smallest element from the remaining elements and places in the first<br />\nremaining location. The process is repeated (n – 1) times.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f716d86b9226e821f7e3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Identify the missing expressions at P and Q respectively.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/20/2023e0d17f861193db5d.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/40/400a617761e7746adad0.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/256d2d210cf2a96226f5.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c206f4d720311f165c65.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/14/1474544f5d82720b262b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following entity set:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5b2d610c1a035f732d38.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>How many minimum RDBMS relations required which satisfy BCNF?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dc995a8fd315e5491fa2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":65,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a two-level cache system, the access time of level-1 is 2 clock cycles and level-2 access time is 11 clock cycles, the miss penalty from the level-2 cache to main memory is 40 clock cycles. If the miss rate of level-1 cache is twice that of level-2 cache, miss rate of level-1 is 0.28, and each <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> miss also incurs an average dirty-evict cost of 6 clock cycles (paid during the miss), then the average memory access time for the system is ________ clock cycles. (Upto 3 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8.328,8.328],"answerText":"8.328","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8.328</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/df/df640fd93f0b5a42ef9b.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":46,"MSQ":6,"NAT":13},"withoutKey":0}};
